WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:12.805
Hey, Little Fox readers.

2
00:00:13.545 --> 00:00:14.666
It's me again, Jake.

3
00:00:15.527 --> 00:00:16.988
And I've got some questions for you.

4
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:21.933
What is the world's smallest continent and sixth-largest country?

5
00:00:22.854 --> 00:00:26.797
Where can you find wild kangaroos, koalas, and wombats?

6
00:00:27.758 --> 00:00:30.504
That's right—Australia!

7
00:00:30.524 --> 00:00:33.807
Last year my parents went to Australia for their wedding anniversary.

8
00:00:34.708 --> 00:00:39.512
When they came back, they showed me pictures of a cool building in Sydney, the largest city in

9
00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:45.117
Australia. Guess what—the building turned out to be the landmark we studied in Mr.

10
00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:46.518
Fay's class yesterday!

11
00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:51.382
The Sydney Opera House is one of the most photographed buildings in the world.

12
00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:57.747
I found out that it's on the UNESCO World Heritage List, which means it's a very important world

13
00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:03.779
landmark. Most of the other landmarks on the list, like the pyramids of Egypt and the Great Wall

14
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.701
of China, are really ancient.

15
00:01:06.682 --> 00:01:12.786
But the Sydney Opera House, completed in 1973, is the youngest site to be included on the list.

16
00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:19.531
The reason the Sydney Opera House became such a famous landmark is because of its very unusual design.

17
00:01:20.552 --> 00:01:25.616
The roofs were designed to look like billowing sails—perfect for its location in Sydney Harbor.

18
00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:32.450
Mr. Fay said that many people consider the building a modern-day masterpiece!

19
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:38.111
In 1956 the government in Sydney held a competition for the best design of a new performance center.

20
00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:46.474
Over two hundred entries—including one from a Danish architect named Jorn Utzon—came in from all over the world.

21
00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:51.075
According to one story, Utzon's design was almost thrown out.

22
00:01:52.135 --> 00:01:55.036
Luckily it was rescued by a judge who arrived late.

23
00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.164
As the judge was looking through entries discarded by other judges, he found Utzon's design and wondered why

24
00:02:01.204 --> 00:02:02.104
it had been rejected.

25
00:02:03.085 --> 00:02:04.446
He thought it was amazing!

26
00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:11.911
This judge convinced the others to take another look, and on January 29, 1957, Utzon was announced the

27
00:02:11.951 --> 00:02:18.696
winner. Once construction began, Utzon and his team of builders realized that it would be very difficult to

28
00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:20.618
turn the design into a real building.

29
00:02:21.578 --> 00:02:26.289
The shells Utzon wanted to use for the roof turned out to be particularly challenging.

30
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.632
Utzon wanted the shells to be white, like sails or clouds.

31
00:02:31.692 --> 00:02:35.394
But he also didn't want to blind people when the sun reflected off the roof.

32
00:02:36.515 --> 00:02:40.938
Utzon eventually found the perfect inspiration: Japanese ceramic bowls.

33
00:02:42.078 --> 00:02:46.661
The clay used to make these bowls was light-colored but rough enough to keep the bowls from being

34
00:02:46.721 --> 00:02:47.441
too shiny.

35
00:02:48.482 --> 00:02:54.226
He asked a Swedish ceramic company to invent a special tile that was similar to the Japanese bowls.

36
00:02:55.387 --> 00:02:59.990
This new tile, called Sydney tile, was made from clay and crushed stone.

37
00:03:01.572 --> 00:03:05.535
It took over a million tiles and several years to create the shells.

38
00:03:06.596 --> 00:03:12.401
Then in 1965, just after the shells had finally been finished, a new government official was put in

39
00:03:12.441 --> 00:03:13.421
charge of the project.

40
00:03:14.442 --> 00:03:19.566
This official didn't really like the building's design, and he thought the project was too expensive.

41
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.770
Eventually he persuaded the government to stop paying Utzon.

42
00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.072
So in 1966 Utzon quit.

43
00:03:27.953 --> 00:03:30.554
Many people thought the official's actions were unfair.

44
00:03:31.495 --> 00:03:36.358
They held protests and marches on the streets of Sydney, asking the government to give Utzon back his

45
00:03:36.418 --> 00:03:39.220
job. But the government refused.

46
00:03:40.221 --> 00:03:45.424
Utzon and his family returned to Europe, and a new architectural team was chosen to complete the building.

47
00:03:46.725 --> 00:03:52.521
By the time the Sydney Opera House opened in October of 1973, many changes had been made to

48
00:03:52.561 --> 00:03:53.541
the original design.

49
00:03:54.501 --> 00:03:58.523
I'm an artist myself, so I bet Utzon was pretty sad about what happened!

50
00:03:59.503 --> 00:04:02.184
Jorn Utzon wasn't forgotten though.

51
00:04:02.324 --> 00:04:08.567
In 1999, after a series of conversations and meetings, Utzon returned as the opera house's architect.

52
00:04:09.587 --> 00:04:14.889
His first major project was to redo the reception hall, now called the Utzon Room in his honor.

53
00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:20.852
It's the only hall in the whole opera house that is completely true to Utzon's own design.

54
00:04:21.896 --> 00:04:30.343
In 2003 Utzon received the highest honor in international architecture, the Pritzker Prize, for his many accomplishments, including

55
00:04:30.363 --> 00:04:32.725
his imaginative design of the Sydney Opera House.

56
00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:36.188
The Sydney Opera House is really big.

57
00:04:37.069 --> 00:04:43.274
It has seven main performance halls, which include an opera theater, a concert hall, a playhouse, and an

58
00:04:43.454 --> 00:04:44.675
outdoor concert area.

59
00:04:45.596 --> 00:04:50.060
As you can tell from these different halls, the Sydney Opera House isn't just for opera lovers.

60
00:04:51.068 --> 00:04:57.273
Each year there are over 1,500 programs, which feature individuals and groups from all over the world.

61
00:04:58.174 --> 00:05:04.839
Performances include ethnic dance groups, symphony orchestras, rock-and-roll concerts, plays, and ballets.

62
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.942
There are even movies and puppet shows!

63
00:05:09.423 --> 00:05:14.547
Mr. Fay also told us about Le Grande Cirque, an amazing circus that performs at the Sydney Opera

64
00:05:14.587 --> 00:05:20.492
House. Usually I think circuses are for little kids, but this one sounds really awesome.

65
00:05:21.344 --> 00:05:27.047
For one of the acts, performers zoom around on motorcycles inside a huge, ball-shaped cage!

66
00:05:28.208 --> 00:05:30.789
Over a million people attend these events each year.

67
00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.372
But over seven million come to the Sydney Opera House just to see the site.

68
00:05:36.372 --> 00:05:39.894
There are tours offered every day in at least seven different languages.

69
00:05:40.895 --> 00:05:43.436
There is also a two-hour long backstage tour.

70
00:05:44.417 --> 00:05:49.259
My parents said that sometimes visitors are allowed to do cool things, like stand on the stage in

71
00:05:49.279 --> 00:05:51.898
the concert hall or visit the stars' dressing rooms.

72
00:05:53.459 --> 00:05:57.342
When I got home from school, I asked my parents if I could see their photograph of the

73
00:05:57.382 --> 00:05:58.503
Sydney Opera House again.

74
00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:01.526
As soon as I saw it, I had an idea.

75
00:06:02.407 --> 00:06:05.089
I rushed up to my room and drew a picture of the opera house.

76
00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.334
But I added one thing: a statue of Jorn Utzon, sketching by the harbor.

77
00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:12.375
I think it looks great there.

78
00:06:13.216 --> 00:06:13.657
Who knows?

79
00:06:14.617 --> 00:06:17.380
Someday my design could become a real statue!
